!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/parser-sidecar.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a parser-layer sidecar (tests/class1/parser/**/*-expected.tn) -- see README.md's
  "Valid parser-layer vectors" and "Schema-document vectors" sections, and RUNNER.md.

  document/data_value/sidecar_annotation/core_value mirror the spec's own grammar
  vocabulary (§2.3, §7.4), not any implementation's internal AST names, so the schema stays
  language-agnostic the same way the sidecars do.

  Field names match the real sidecar's own spelling exactly, hyphens included (`type-ref`,
  `empty-brace`): a hyphenated field declaration parses and resolves fine, and nothing in the grammar
  requires the snake_case this project uses for *type* names. Type names never appear literally in
  sidecar data, so there is no wire fidelity to preserve for them.
  """
!schema {
  core_value_form => !enum [unquoted single-line multi-line]

  token_core => {
    form: core_value_form
    text: text
  }

  record_core => {
    fields: [record_field_entry]
  }

  map_core => {
    entries: [map_entry]
  }

  array_core => {
    elements: [data_value]
  }

  @doc:"""
    A reference with arguments (§7.4): its qualified name, as one text, and each argument, a reference
    of its own or a token. A qualified name without arguments is a token.
    """
  reference_core => {
    name: text
    arguments: [reference_argument]
  }

  @doc:"One argument of a reference: a nested reference with arguments, or a token."
  reference_argument => {
    ( reference: reference_core
    | token: token_core )
  }

  @doc:"One field of a record core-value."
  record_field_entry => {
    name: text
    value: data_value
  }

  @doc:"One entry of a map core-value."
  map_entry => {
    key: data_value
    value: data_value
  }

  @doc:"""
    One core-value (§2.3), as exactly one of the seven kinds. A REQUIRED field group (§5.11), so a
    token carries form and text and cannot carry `fields`, a record carries `fields` and nothing
    else, and so on -- the correlation the flat `kind` discriminator could state but not enforce.

    `absent` and `empty-brace` have no payload, so they are typed `void?` -- `void`, whose only value
    is the void sentinel, at a position that admits it: they are written `absent: _` and
    `empty-brace: _`. §2.8's empty
    brace is deliberately still unresolved here -- which container it denotes is a reader question,
    not a Tier 3 one.
    """
  core_value => {
    ( token: token_core
    | reference: reference_core
    | record: record_core
    | map: map_core
    | array: array_core
    | absent: void?
    | empty-brace: void? )
  }

  @doc:"A data-value (§2.3): zero or more annotations, an optional type-ref, and exactly one core-value. Every value position holds one: the root, a record field value, a map key or entry value, an array element."
  data_value => {
    annotations: [sidecar_annotation]
    type-ref?: text?
    core: core_value
  }

  @doc:"""
    One annotation (§3.1) -- a name and an optional value, itself a full data-value. Named
    sidecar_annotation rather than annotation because core.tn's namespace already binds that name
    (its own @annotation-tagged marker atom), and an import may not shadow one.
    """
  sidecar_annotation => {
    name: text
    value?: data_value?
  }

  @doc:"The parsed document (§2.3, §7.4): the header's own `!!id`, absent as `_` when not present, its `!!import`s in order, empty when there are none, plus the root data-value."
  document => {
    id?: text?
    imports: [text]
    root: data_value
  }

  parser_valid => {
    document: document
  }

  @doc:"""
    Exactly one of the three outcomes. `schema-document` is a document whose root is tagged `!schema`:
    §1.5 makes rejecting it with a categorized diagnostic a Class 1 obligation, and there is nothing
    further for the vector to state, so the member is `void?` and written `schema-document: _`.
    """
  parser_sidecar => sidecar_common & {
    ( valid: parser_valid
    | error: vector_error
    | schema-document: void? )
  }
}
